# Generics & Traits

## Traits: shared behavior contracts

```rust
trait Summary {
    fn summarize(&self) -> String;
    fn preview(&self) -> String {           // default implementation
        format!("{}…", self.summarize().chars().take(20).collect::<String>())
    }
}

struct Article { title: String }
impl Summary for Article {
    fn summarize(&self) -> String { self.title.clone() }
}
```

## Generic functions with bounds

```rust
fn announce<T: Summary>(item: &T) {
    println!("Breaking: {}", item.summarize());
}

fn largest<T: PartialOrd>(items: &[T]) -> &T {
    let mut best = &items[0];
    for item in items { if item > best { best = item; } }
    best
}
```

Monomorphization: the compiler stamps out a copy per concrete type → zero-cost
abstraction.

## Three ways to say "some Summary"

| Form | When |
| --- | --- |
| `<T: Summary>` | Multiple related generic params, need the type name |
| `impl Summary` param/return | One input position, simple signatures |
| `&dyn Summary` | Heterogeneous collections / dynamic dispatch (tiny cost) |

Associated types (`type Item`) let traits define placeholders each implementor
ties down once — you'll meet them with `Iterator`.

examples.code:
trait Area {
    fn area(&self) -> f64;
}

struct Square(f64);
struct Circle(f64);

impl Area for Square {
    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.0 }
}
impl Area for Circle {
    fn area(&self) -> f64 { std::f64::consts::PI * self.0 * self.0 }
}

fn total<T: Area>(shapes: &[T]) -> f64 {
    shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum()
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", total(&[Square(2.0)]));
    println!("{}", total(&[Circle(1.0), Circle(2.0)]));
}

